  "apod": {
    "date": "2026-11-07",
    "title": "De Gerlache-Kocher Massif",
    "explanation": " Explanation:  De Gerlache-Kocher  massif  looms on the visible horizon in this close-up of the edge of the Moon. The mountainous formation rises about 4 kilometers above its surroundings and is located very close to the lunar south pole. Boasting  permanently shadowed  crater floors that likely contain deposits of water ice and peaks that receive almost continuous sunlight for solar power generation, the south polar region is consider a prime location for the first human settlement on the Moon.  In fact , De Gerlache-Kocher massif itself has been consider as a potential  landing site  for future crewed  Artemis missions to the Moon . The remarkable telescopic image of the massif at the lunar limb was captured on October 31. Then, a favorable libration angle or  lunar wobble  enhanced this oblique view  across the Moon's south pole.    Tomorrow's picture:  meteoric",
    "image": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/GerlacheKocherMassif_Sans.jpg?w=1500&h=1022&fit=clip&crop=faces,focalpoint",
    "link": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-7-de-gerlache-kocher-massif/",
    "credit": " Alejandro Sanz ",
    "alt": "Mountains and craters at the lunar limb.",
    "media_type": "image"
  },
  "updated": "2026-11-07T08:18:05.943439+00:00"
